its in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"TTHM":{"plain":"Trihalomethanes are not added to water. They form when the chlorine used to disinfect it reacts with natural organic matter already present. They are a by-product of making water safe from bacteria.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing trihalomethanes in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could experience problems with their liver, kidneys, or central nervous system, and may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Total Trihalomethanes","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"TTHM"},"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)":{"plain":"Like trihalomethanes, haloacetic acids form when disinfectant reacts with organic matter in the water. They are a side-effect of disinfection, not an added ingredient.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing haloacetic acids in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Haloacetic Acids","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)"}}}